0000:                   ********************************************************************
0000:                   * Setime - Set Date/Time
0000:                   *
0000:                   * $Id$
0000:                   *
0000:                   * This is a Setime for use when a Y2K compliant system is required.
0000:                   * 
0000:                   * This is somewhat of a cheat in that any attempts to do a full
0000:                   * 4 digit conversion on the entered date string would have
0000:                   * seriously broken the nice, generally compact codeing of the
0000:                   * original setime module.  I didn't want to do that, so I cheated
0000:                   * and simply added one more byte to the date packet image it builds
0000:                   * on the stack.  Its conversion algorithm does 2 digits per pass/byte
0000:                   * so if you enter 1998 (You MUST now enter the year as a 4 digit
0000:                   * value!), this image will have a $13 as itas first and unused
0000:                   * digit, if you enter a 20xx date, it will be a $14.
0000:                   * 
0000:                   * I check this value after the rest of the conversion is all
0000:                   * done, and if its $14 (or more), an extra 100 ($64) is added to the
0000:                   * os9 year byte before the system F$STime call is made, pointing
0000:                   * the F$STime call at only the normal 6 byte packet of time data.
0000:                   * 
0000:                   * I've played with this maybe 20 minutes.  Hopefully you can use
0000:                   * this into the next century without any further concerns about
0000:                   * the "Y2K" problem.
0000:                   *
0000:                   * Edt/Rev  YYYY/MM/DD  Modified by
0000:                   * Comment
0000:                   * ------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:                   *  10      ????/??/??  Gene Heskett
0000:                   * Made Y2K compliant.
0000:                   *
0000:                   *  11      1999/05/07  Boisy G. Pitre
0000:                   * Made totally compliant for 1900-2155.
0000:                   *
0000:                   *  12      2002/07/20  Boisy G. Pitre
0000:                   * Typing a CR at the prompt no longer sets the time to a bogus value.
0000:                   
0000:                            nam   Setime
0000:                            ttl   Set Date/Time
0000:                   
0000:                   * Disassembled 96/09/26 00:34:54 by Dsm v1.7 (C) 1988 by RML
0000:                   
0000:                            ifp1  
0000:                            use   defsfile
0000:                            endc  
0000:                   
